# Chapter Nine — For Tomorrow

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.3. Cut by a third after the pass-2 review: Mrs. Vale's rules and the second carton reduced, Ruth's telephone call to Ward taken off the page. The eggs and the car are untouched. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 22 — Mrs. Vale's key

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ruth, and Mrs. Vale.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth is Paul's next of kin, Nell can identify June's property, and Mrs. Vale is the person who controls the door.

INT. THE KENTON ROOMS, SOUTH ALVARADO - AFTERNOON

At three ten, Ward's officer collected Lydia's archive with her and Nell's written consent and left neither woman with a piece of it.

The Kenton Rooms occupies the second and third floors above a pharmacy on South Alvarado. At four Thursday afternoon, Mrs. Vale meets Henry, Nell, and Ruth at the top of the stairs. She is a small woman in a gray house dress with a ring of keys clipped at her waist. The paper police seal on Room 26 has been cut; she keeps the loose half folded inside a copy of the search warrant.

MRS. VALE
Miss Ellery.

RUTH
Ruth, please.

MRS. VALE
The police called you Miss Ellery.

She looks at Nell and Henry.

MRS. VALE
Which one is family?

RUTH
I am his sister. Nell was his wife.

NELL
Former wife.

MRS. VALE
And you?

HENRY
Henry Calder. Nell retained me to identify property that may have belonged to her mother.

MRS. VALE
You don't open anything that is shut and you don't put anything in your pockets. I stay in the room. The police took the letters, his appointment book, his check register, and the loose typed pages from the desk. If you came for one of those, it isn't here.

She unlocks the door.

The room is narrower than Henry's office and better kept. A bed fills one wall. A table stands beneath the window. On it, a small electric fan has been taken apart in an orderly row over a folded towel.

There is a hot plate, a sink, one saucepan, and a black frying pan with a worn wooden handle. Two battered household cartons sit open beneath the table. Small numbered cards left by the police remain beside both.

Nell points at the frying pan.

NELL
That is mine.

RUTH
We have been inside ten seconds.

NELL
It is still mine.

MRS. VALE
He used it under the sink.

NELL
For cooking?

MRS. VALE
For the drip. The cold tap ran brown.

Ruth looks at Nell.

RUTH
He was right about the water.

NELL
Once.

Mrs. Vale opens the curtain. The window faces the wall of the next building. She moves her chair to where she can watch both women.

## Scene 23 — The two boxes

INT. ROOM 26, THE KENTON ROOMS - AFTERNOON

The first carton has PAUL — PAPERS written across one side in black marker. Under the words, half-covered by brown tape, is an older label in Marian's hand:

!JUNE — WORK.

Nell kneels without touching the box.

NELL
This was in our hall closet. The ring on the lid. I put a wet glass there and Marian made me sand it. It only made the ring larger.

Inside are household receipts, old envelopes, several wardrobe patterns, and a stack of Paul's bills folded into thirds. Along one side are six empty cardboard sleeves sized for small tape reels. They are numbered in Lydia's hand.

There is a pale rectangle where a folder once lay. One strip of blue paper remains beneath the edge of a divider.

NELL
The reels and folder were here.

HENRY
They could have been.

NELL
Robert saw them yesterday.

Ruth reads the covered label.

RUTH
Paul brought this box to my apartment the night he left you.

NELL
You saw the label.

RUTH
I saw two boxes. It was after midnight. He said they were his papers.

NELL
And you believed him.

RUTH
I believed the part that made the night easier.

MRS. VALE
You are all using "mine" differently. I need one of you to tell me what I write on the inventory.

HENRY
Write what is on the box. Ward holds it until somebody decides.

He looks at Ruth, then Nell. Both say yes.

MRS. VALE
Then I write both names.

The second carton holds tax forms, appliance instructions, and several years of paid bills. Nothing in it bears June's name. Mrs. Vale checks the police card beside it.

MRS. VALE
The detectives took the check register from behind the file. They photographed the rest where it lay.

The top paper is the carbon of an invoice. Nobody moves it.

!P. ELLERY — CONTINUITY RESEARCH

!ACCOUNT 418-R — SECOND DELIVERY

## Scene 24 — 418-R

INT. ROOM 26, THE KENTON ROOMS - AFTERNOON

The number on the invoice is the same one Lydia's caller used to ask about *Private Weather*.

HENRY
418-R.

RUTH
I have seen it before.

Nell looks at her.

NELL
Where?

Ruth looks at the six empty sleeves. Her hands remain inside her coat pockets.

RUTH
On checks from Paul.

NELL
For what?

RUTH
Dialogue. He brought pages without a title or names. I revised them. He took them away and paid me.

NELL
How many times?

Ruth takes one hand out of her pocket. She does not count the sleeves.

RUTH
More than three.

HENRY
Did you ever ask what picture it was?

RUTH
On the third set, I asked what it was called. Paul said the title would change.

NELL
Did you ask again?

RUTH
No.

NELL
And that was not the last set.

RUTH
No.

HENRY
Did you know *Private Weather*?

RUTH
No. What is it?

NELL
The picture my mother worked on. The sentence Lydia said at supper is in my mother's handwriting in the continuity book.

Ruth sits on the edge of the chair Mrs. Vale is not using.

RUTH
I never saw that title on a page.

NELL
You were paid from her work.

RUTH
I did not know it was hers.

NELL
You knew Paul had not written it.

RUTH
Yes.

Mrs. Vale lays her telephone-message carbon book beside the visitor ledger. It remains her hand on the page.

MRS. VALE
The police copied these. This name called more than once.

The latest entry was made Wednesday at 3:14 p.m. It reads:

!M. HAVEL — 418-R — THURSDAY, 10:00 A.M.

!TREMONT ANNEX

MRS. VALE
Bungalow Six. The caller said to ask for it at the gate.

HENRY
The message at Lydia's had M.H. and the same code.

He looks at Ruth.

HENRY
Do you know Havel?

RUTH
No.

Henry writes the address in his notebook.

HENRY
You need to tell Ward about the checks.

RUTH
I know.

Mrs. Vale points toward the hall telephone. Ruth goes alone and leaves the door open. Nell and Mrs. Vale hear her tell Ward that work Paul brought her was connected to 418-R, and then her voice goes too low to follow. When she comes back she gives the receiver to Henry, and he reports the covered label, the sleeves, the strip of blue, the invoice, and the matched message, and asks for an officer to collect the disputed carton.

HENRY
Ward has the match now. He will speak to Havel before we do.

NELL
And after?

HENRY
Afterward, we ask for your mother's property.

Mrs. Vale closes neither book.

## Scene 25 — For tomorrow

INT. ROOM 26, THE KENTON ROOMS - AFTERNOON

On the counter beside the sink are bread, coffee, and two eggs in a paper carton. The receipt beneath them is stamped Wednesday, 8:11 a.m.

MRS. VALE
There was milk too. I put it in my refrigerator after the police left.

NELL
He bought food and forgot to eat it.

MRS. VALE
Before he went out last night, he asked me whether the grocer would be open in the morning.

No one answers that.

Mrs. Vale goes to the table. The fan's screws, guard, blades, and motor are laid out in separate rows. A small paper packet rests beside them.

MRS. VALE
This is mine. It stopped turning last week.

RUTH
Paul said he could fix anything with one moving part.

MRS. VALE
He said two.

She opens the packet. A new metal washer lies inside.

NELL
Was he paid through the month?

MRS. VALE
Five days late. Paid through next month.

She looks around the room.

MRS. VALE
He carried Mrs. Alvarez's groceries. He could avoid me for three days in one hallway. I liked him enough to be angry.

Ruth looks at the good frying pan beneath the sink.

RUTH
He used that the first morning he stayed with me.

NELL
For eggs?

RUTH
Capers. He said they improved them.

NELL
They don't.

RUTH
No.

Mrs. Vale returns the washer to its packet.

RUTH
Why didn't he finish the fan?

MRS. VALE
He said Sunday.

The dismantled fan remains in its careful rows.

## Scene 26 — Alvarado

= Present: Henry and Ruth, in Henry's car.

= Immediate pressure: none that Ruth will admit to. Henry is driving a woman home who has just learned what her brother was paying her from.

INT. HENRY'S CAR, ALVARADO STREET - EVENING

At twenty to six, Ward's officer carries the disputed carton down the stairs of the Kenton Rooms with Mrs. Vale's receipt folded on top of it. Nell takes a cab from the corner without asking who is coming with her.

Henry's car is parked in front of the pharmacy. Ruth stands beside it.

RUTH
I don't want to be walked to a streetcar.

HENRY
Get in.

He drives. She gives him her street in three words and no others. Alvarado runs south under the last of the light. Sprinklers are on in front of a bank.

RUTH
He bought two eggs.

HENRY
Yes.

RUTH
Not a dozen. Two. He was going to come back and eat two eggs.

HENRY
With capers.

RUTH
He didn't have any capers. I looked.

She looks out at the bank.

RUTH
That is what I did in that room. I looked for capers.

Henry drives.

RUTH
He couldn't put a fan back together. He could take one apart. He laid the screws out in a row so that whoever finished it would know the order. That was the arrangement. He took things apart very carefully and left the rest for somebody who—

She stops. She does not start the sentence again.

HENRY
Somebody like you.

RUTH
Somebody like anyone.

They pass under the freeway. It is loud enough that neither of them has to speak until it is behind them.

RUTH
Do you know what the last thing he said to me was?

HENRY
No.

RUTH
"Help me."

Henry keeps his eyes on the road.

RUTH
Not "help me tell her." Not "help me with the words." Just that. And I said no.

HENRY
You had told him you would not explain him.

RUTH
I know what I had told him.

Her building is a pale stucco block with a courtyard and a dry fountain. Henry pulls in beside it and turns off the engine. Ruth does not open the door.

RUTH
I keep thinking I'll go up and he'll have let himself in with the key he doesn't have.

HENRY
He gave it to you in May.

RUTH
That was the other key.

She laughs once. It is not a good sound and she does not apologize for it.

RUTH
I have to call a funeral home. I have to find out whether the police will release him, and to whom, and what a sister is entitled to sign. I know how to find that out. I wrote the pamphlet for one of them. "At a time like this."

HENRY
Do you want me to—

RUTH
No.

She opens the door and does not get out.

RUTH
Thank you for not saying anything in the car.

HENRY
I said several things.

RUTH
None of them were about me.

She gets out. Henry watches her cross the courtyard, find her key on the first try, and go in without looking back.

He sits in the car until the light in a second-floor window comes on, and then a while longer, because it does not go off.
